# Secrets — never commit. The credentials panel refuses to write anywhere git tracks, so this
# file is what makes `python -m hawedit.credentials` able to store a key at all. It lived in
# the host repository's root .gitignore before the split and did not come with it.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
secrets/
*.pem

.venv/
.venv-wsl/
/.venv-*/
/.serena/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
*.egg-info/
/work/
/out/
/corpus/audio/
.ffmpeg/
# Weights never enter the repository. `sources.json` is not weights — it is the few dozen bytes
# that say which repository each §7 checkpoint comes from, and it must be the same on every
# checkout. Ignored, it had to be re-derived per machine, and re-deriving it is precisely the
# guessing D-022 forbids. `models/*` rather than `models/`, because git cannot re-include a
# file whose parent *directory* is excluded.
models/*
!models/sources.json
# Same reasoning, same trap: `revisions.json` pins the commit each repository is downloaded at
# (D-073). Ignored, it existed on the machine that wrote it and nowhere else — which is how it
# first shipped: `git add -A` skipped it in silence, the local gate passed against a file CI
# never received, and the runner failed on `no pinned revisions found`.
!models/revisions.json
# Content-addressed identities for those exact revisions (D-096), not weight bytes.
!models/integrity.json
/.gate/
/build/
/dist/
# DBOS's own system database (D-A3): local SQLite by default, one file, never committed — it
# is process state (workflow/step checkpoints), not source, and its schema is DBOS's to
# migrate, not this repo's to track.
/.dbos/
# The judge promotion ledger (D-A14): installation-level state (which judge version is
# currently active), not source — the same reasoning as /.dbos/ above.
/.hawedit/
